<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rheingrafenstein</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rheingrafenstein is a ruined castle on a rocky crag above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahe">Nahe River</a> near Bad Münster am Stein-Ebernburg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Built in the 11th or 12th century, it was the seat of the Rhinegraves, who took their name from it, until they inherited the Wildgraviate in 1409. French troops destroyed the castle in 1689 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a>.</p></div>
